      <div id="live-stories" class="dash__stories">
                  <div class="dash__story" data-beat="geopolitics">
            <div class="dash__story-meta">
                            <span class="dash__story-source"><a href="https://www.scmp.com/tech/big-tech/article/3361109/how-chinese-tech-giants-ant-tencent-use-ai-agents-win-over-enterprise-clients" target="_blank" rel="noopener" style="color:inherit;">scmp.com</a></span>
              <span class="dash__story-time">0m ago</span>
                            <span class="dash__story-score">21</span>
            </div>
                                      <a href="/node/7280" rel="noopener" class="dash__story-headline">Ant Group ships Agentar 2.0 with 200 templates as WAIC agent race heats up</a>
                        <div class="dash__story-sum">At Shanghai&#039;s WAIC, Ant Group&#039;s Ant Digital Technologies launched Agentar 2.0 with 200 pre-configured &#039;digital expert templates&#039; and hundreds of out-of-the-box agent tools, positioning it as workflow-restructuring rather than task-acceleration. Tencent, Alibaba and Baidu also fielded competing enterprise-agent pitches at the four-day conference. SCMP frames it as Chinese big tech racing to win B2B agent adoption against US rivals.</div>          </div>
                  <div class="dash__story" data-beat="research">
            <div class="dash__story-meta">
                            <span class="dash__story-source"><a href="https://thenextweb.com/news/ai-advice-suppresses-critical-thinking-wrong-answers-study" target="_blank" rel="noopener" style="color:inherit;">thenextweb.com</a></span>
              <span class="dash__story-time">0m ago</span>
                            <span class="dash__story-score">22</span>
            </div>
                                      <a href="/node/7279" rel="noopener" class="dash__story-headline">AI advice tanks accuracy from 27% to 9%, but confidence soars</a>
                        <div class="dash__story-sum">A study by Valerio Capraro (Milano-Bicocca), Chiara Marcoccia (ENS) and Walter Quattrociocchi (Sapienza) using Step 3.5 Flash finds AI advice drops user accuracy from 27% to 9% and &#039;I don&#039;t know&#039; responses from 44% to 3%, while confidence climbs from 30% to 76%. Wharton is cited putting the &#039;cognitive surrender&#039; rate at ~80%. TheNextWeb frames it as evidence AI advice systematically degrades decision quality even as it makes users feel more certain.</div>          </div>
                  <div class="dash__story" data-beat="workforce">
            <div class="dash__story-meta">
                            <span class="dash__story-source"><a href="https://www.wsj.com/cio-journal/meet-the-companies-shelling-out-for-top-ai-models-e1fe3375" target="_blank" rel="noopener" style="color:inherit;">wsj.com</a></span>
              <span class="dash__story-time">2h ago</span>
                            <span class="dash__story-score">21</span>
            </div>
                                      <a href="/alerts/wsj-some-firms-still-shelling-out-for-top-tier-ai-models" rel="noopener" class="dash__story-headline">Shopify bars engineers from cheaper AI models, WSJ says</a>
                        <div class="dash__story-sum">WSJ CIO Journal reports Shopify is mandating frontier AI models across engineering, forbidding engineers from using cheaper alternatives, even as many enterprises move the other direction and switch workloads to smaller, cheaper models to cut AI bills. The piece frames enterprise AI strategy as splitting into two camps: cost-cutters routing to distilled and open-weight models, and quality-maximizers paying up for Opus/GPT-tier reasoning. Fetch was blocked by wsj.com paywall, so specifics beyond the Techmeme summary are unconfirmed.</div>          </div>
                  <div class="dash__story" data-beat="markets">
            <div class="dash__story-meta">
                            <span class="dash__story-source"><a href="https://www.nytimes.com/2026/07/18/business/world-cup-kalshi-polymarket.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener" style="color:inherit;">nytimes.com</a></span>
              <span class="dash__story-time">3h ago</span>
                            <span class="dash__story-score">16</span>
            </div>
                                      <a href="/alerts/kalshi-and-polymarket-cross-50b-in-june-as-world-cup-drives-bets" rel="noopener" class="dash__story-headline">Kalshi and Polymarket clear $50B on World Cup markets</a>
                        <div class="dash__story-sum">The New York Times reports that combined Kalshi and Polymarket wagers on the FIFA World Cup final have topped $5.69B, and Dune Analytics data cited by the story says total volume on the two prediction markets crossed $50B for the first time in June. Kalshi has become a growing AI-adjacent data source, with OpenAI recently integrating Kalshi odds into ChatGPT Search.</div>          </div>
                  <div class="dash__story" data-beat="builders">
            <div class="dash__story-meta">
                            <span class="dash__story-source">x.com</span>
              <span class="dash__story-time">4h ago</span>
                            <span class="dash__story-score">26</span>
            </div>
                                      <a href="https://x.com/kimi_moonshot/status/2078855608565207130" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="dash__story-headline">Moonshot pauses Kimi K3 subscriptions as GPUs hit capacity</a>
                        <div class="dash__story-sum">Moonshot posted on X on July 19 that Kimi K3 &#039;received far more love than we expected, and our GPUs are feeling it,&#039; with demand over the past 48 hours pushing close to capacity. To protect existing subscribers, the lab is temporarily pausing new subscriptions while it adds GPU capacity, and plans to split membership into two tiers going forward — Kimi Membership (Web/App/Work) and Kimi Code Membership for coding workflows.</div>          </div>
                  <div class="dash__story" data-beat="research">
            <div class="dash__story-meta">
                            <span class="dash__story-source"><a href="https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2026/jul/19/could-ai-be-conscious" target="_blank" rel="noopener" style="color:inherit;">theguardian.com</a></span>
              <span class="dash__story-time">6h ago</span>
                            <span class="dash__story-score">17</span>
            </div>
                                      <a href="/alerts/macaskill-and-caviola-dont-dismiss-ai-moral-patienthood" rel="noopener" class="dash__story-headline">Guardian: prepare now for the possibility of conscious AI</a>
                        <div class="dash__story-sum">Philosophers William MacAskill and Lucius Caviola argue in The Guardian that although no one knows whether current AI systems are conscious, modern models already match rodent-brain complexity and could approach human-brain scale within 5-10 years. They cite Anthropic&#039;s constitution admitting it can&#039;t rule out Claude moral patienthood, David Chalmers&#039;s estimate of a significant chance of conscious LLMs within a decade, and Claude test responses that put its own probability of being a moral patient at 5-40%.</div>          </div>
