                                      <a href="/alerts/openai-codex-cuts-gpt-56-context-window-from-372k-to-272k" rel="noopener" class="dash__story-headline">OpenAI drops Codex context from 372k to 272k, charges 2x over cutoff</a>
                        <div class="dash__story-sum">A July 19 Codex PR ships bundled model metadata that caps the effective GPT-5.6 context window at 272,000 input tokens plus 128,000 reserved for output, down from 372K previously. OpenAI now bills requests above the 272K input threshold at 2x input and 1.5x output pricing for the full call, and developers on HN and X are flagging that many harnesses still assume 372K and will burn quota faster than expected until they reset.</div>          </div>
                  <div class="dash__story" data-beat="workforce">
            <div class="dash__story-meta">
                            <span class="dash__story-source">marketscale.com</span>
              <span class="dash__story-time">11h ago</span>
                            <span class="dash__story-score">22</span>
            </div>
                                      <a href="https://www.marketscale.com/industries/software-and-technology/only-11-of-sp-500-firms-have-deeply-integrated-ai-mit-study-finds" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="dash__story-headline">Only 11% of S&amp;P 500 firms deeply integrate AI, and most are already tech</a>
                        <div class="dash__story-sum">A new MIT FutureTech working paper by Yang Yu, Martin Fleming, Lucy Hampton, Christophe Combemale and Neil Thompson finds only 11% of S&amp;P 500 firms had deeply integrated AI into core operations as of 2025 — quadrupling from 5% in 2022 but still narrow, with two-thirds of that integration concentrated in tech companies. The authors used SEC 10-K disclosures rather than surveys and warn that firms moving from no AI to deep integration typically see a profitability dip before gains, driven by model cost, org restructuring and error-propagation risk in supply chains.</div>          </div>
